  "title": "More from Less: 3D Gaussian Splatting Digital Twins with Iterative Expansion and Trajectory Compensation for Sparse-Demonstration Bimanual Manipulation",
  "abstract_en": "Bimanual manipulation requires both arms to coordinate their motion during contact. Collecting demonstrations is costly, so training data cover only a fraction of the interaction states the robot may encounter. We propose MFL (More from Less), which expands training data in a digital twin and compensates for local deviations during deployment. A vision–language–action (VLA) policy learns to predict both arms' actions from real demonstrations using a shared task context and geometric supervision. The twin represents the background and each manipulable object as separate 3D Gaussian splatting models and uses independently specified collision geometry and physical parameters. The policy starts candidate rollouts from feasible states near observed failures. Complete trajectories that achieve the task and pass physics and safety checks supplement real demonstrations, which retain the greater weight during retraining. The updated policy identifies where to sample next. During delay-tolerant recovery, a risk-threshold crossing, stage transition or local failure can trigger asynchronous video generation. A state-aligned 2D track from the video guides the policy action only when risk is high and the mapped joint reference is valid. All actions pass through the same safety filter. On six tabletop tasks designed for our SO101 bimanual robot, including pouring and board wiping, MFL achieved 89.00% four-stage macro-average success, 13.17 percentage points above the structured VLA alone. In a separate evaluation, full compensation recovered 67.78% of matched trigger segments.",
  "source_tex_sha256": "245fcbfdabadcf633cdd52dcf3e0dee643a240daae09c9f8e16767ca10494034",
